    "html": "<p>Six cells is enough for a focused command center — if you arrange them with\nintent. Here are patterns that work.</p>\n<h2>1. Put your inbox top-left</h2>\n<p>We read screens top-left first. Give that spot to whatever you check most —\nusually email or chat.</p>\n<h2>2. Group by rhythm, not by app type</h2>\n<p>Cluster the things you glance at constantly (calendar, tasks) apart from the\nthings you dip into occasionally (docs, analytics).</p>\n<h2>3. Keep one “focus” cell</h2>\n<p>Reserve a cell for whatever you are actively working in today, and swap it as\nyour focus changes.</p>\n<h2>4. Use the companion sparingly</h2>\n<p>Only unlock the sites you truly need composited. Fewer heavy frames means a\nsnappier grid.</p>\n<h2>5. Let quiet cells sleep</h2>\n<p>TulipLot unmounts cells you are not looking at to save memory. Put rarely-used\napps where they can rest.</p>\n<p>Small layout choices compound. Spend five minutes arranging, save an hour of\nfriction.</p>\n"
