    "readingMinutes": 1,
    "html": "<p>Most of us spend the workday inside a handful of web apps — email, a calendar,\na project board, chat, a dashboard or two. Yet we access them through a\nsprawling, ever-shifting wall of browser tabs.</p>\n<h2>The cost of tab-hunting</h2>\n<p>Every context switch has a price: the second spent finding the right tab, the\nfocus lost re-orienting, the tab that quietly reloaded and logged you out.\nMultiply that across a day and the drag is real.</p>\n<h2>A fixed place for everything</h2>\n<p>TulipLot replaces the tab wall with a stable 3×2 grid. Each app has a home. You\narrange it once and your eyes learn where things are. Nothing moves unless you\nmove it.</p>\n<h2>Built for the real web</h2>\n<p>The hard part is that the web actively resists this — most sites refuse to be\nembedded. We solve it with a hybrid model: composite what we can directly, and\nuse an optional Chrome companion to unlock the rest, always scoped narrowly and\nalways under your control.</p>\n<p>That is TulipLot: one window, your whole workday.</p>\n"
